  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 335 AM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Slightly cooler today then above normal temperatures return \nThursday through Friday. Uncertainty remains for the potential \nfor rain this weekend. \n\nEarly morning weather analysis weak upper level ridge over west-\ncentral Gulf coast to the Great Lakes and an upper trough over the \nNW CONUS. At the surface, a 1034mb high pressure sits over New \nEngland . The pressure gradient over the area remains weak allowing \nfor weak onshore flow. Temperatures and dew points are much higher \nthan the previous due to the onshore flow and are ranging between \nthe upper 40s to low 50s. In addition, due to clear skies, higher \ndews, and light winds low stratus and patchy fog has developed \nacross SE VA and NE NC. Through the rest of the morning the stratus \nand patchy fog will develop across the piedmont possibly causing \nreduced visibilities. An SPS has been issued at this time due \nto visibilities dropping below a mile across southern and \ninterior southeast Virginia and interior NE North Carolina. \nHowever, if conditions worsen across the area through the early \nmorning the SPS maybe expanded or Dense Fog may potentially be \nissued.\n\n\nFor today, the low stratus is expected to hang on across the piedmont \nas a CAD builds across the area. This will likely cause temperatures \nto struggle to get out of the middle to upper 60s as the SE the \ncloud cover is expected to break early allowing for temps to warm \nnear 70. If the could deck across the piedmont breaks early a run \nnear the 70s cannot be ruled out in the piedmont. However, the \nconfidence is low as the NAM (handles CAD well) is holding onto \nmuch low temps. The high will then eventually anchor off the \nsouthern New England to Mid-Atlantic coast tonight and Thursday \nwhile low pressure remains suppressed well south near the Bahamas. \nWith the high pressure offshore, SSW will allow for highs to \npotentially reach the the middle to upper 70s Thursday and Friday. \n\nDry weather conditions are forecasted to prevail through the late \nweekend, as upper ridging builds over the region. By Friday and into \nthe weekend, a northern stream shortwave traversing central Canada \nwill flatten the upper pattern. Halloween continues to look dry \npleasant, and mild with increasing clouds ahead of of a weak cold \nfront allowing for optimal Trick-or-Treating conditions across the \nregion. Looking Monday into early next week the potential for the \npattern to become slightly unsettled continues to remain low. The \nlatest 00z/28 ensembles continue to hint on a weak system moving \nacross the area during the first couple days of November. However, \nat this time the ensembles continue to remain in  disagreement on \namounts of precipitation and or if there will be any precip. The \nGEFS and Canadian ensembles have roughly 30-50% of rainfall greater \nthan .1\" while the GFS-AI, EURO-AI, and the EURO has little to no \nrain chances. With the disagreement this is continuing to lead to \nlow confidence in any beneficial rainfall at the start of November \nand into early next week.",
